WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:05.571
Hello, everyone! I'm Ms. Sarah. Welcome to Online Advanced.

2
00:00:05.871 --> 00:00:09.095
Hi! I'm Rithy. Let's learn together!

3
00:00:09.395 --> 00:00:16.749
Unit fifty: Media Ownership. In this unit, you will discuss who owns the media and why it matters.

4
00:00:17.750 --> 00:00:19.517
media conglomerate.

5
00:00:20.217 --> 00:00:26.234
media conglomerate means a large company owning many newspapers, TV channels and websites.

6
00:00:26.534 --> 00:00:30.565
A single media conglomerate controls several major channels.

7
00:00:31.265 --> 00:00:34.823
Your turn. Say: media conglomerate.

8
00:00:37.523 --> 00:00:39.093
media conglomerate.

9
00:00:40.083 --> 00:00:42.408
editorial independence.

10
00:00:43.108 --> 00:00:49.543
editorial independence means the freedom of journalists to decide content without outside pressure.

11
00:00:49.843 --> 00:00:52.806
Editorial independence protects honest reporting.

12
00:00:53.506 --> 00:00:57.226
Your turn. Say: editorial independence.

13
00:00:59.926 --> 00:01:01.763
editorial independence.

14
00:01:02.750 --> 00:01:04.436
public broadcaster.

15
00:01:05.136 --> 00:01:11.315
public broadcaster means a TV or radio service funded for the public, not for profit.

16
00:01:11.615 --> 00:01:14.985
The BBC is a well-known public broadcaster.

17
00:01:15.685 --> 00:01:19.127
Your turn. Say: public broadcaster.

18
00:01:21.827 --> 00:01:23.362
public broadcaster.

19
00:01:24.350 --> 00:01:25.827
media pluralism.

20
00:01:26.527 --> 00:01:30.849
media pluralism means the existence of many different media voices.

21
00:01:31.149 --> 00:01:34.797
Media pluralism gives citizens diverse perspectives.

22
00:01:35.497 --> 00:01:38.950
Your turn. Say: media pluralism.

23
00:01:41.650 --> 00:01:43.104
media pluralism.

24
00:01:44.117 --> 00:01:45.222
state media.

25
00:01:45.922 --> 00:01:49.803
state media means media owned or controlled by the government.

26
00:01:50.103 --> 00:01:53.344
State media often present the official view.

27
00:01:54.044 --> 00:01:57.277
Your turn. Say: state media.

28
00:01:59.977 --> 00:02:01.211
state media.

29
00:02:02.217 --> 00:02:03.938
advertiser influence.

30
00:02:04.638 --> 00:02:09.934
advertiser influence means pressure on media content from companies that buy adverts.

31
00:02:10.234 --> 00:02:13.511
Advertiser influence can discourage critical stories.

32
00:02:14.211 --> 00:02:18.001
Your turn. Say: advertiser influence.

33
00:02:20.701 --> 00:02:22.573
advertiser influence.

34
00:02:23.583 --> 00:02:24.782
press ownership.

35
00:02:25.482 --> 00:02:30.198
press ownership means who owns newspapers and news organisations.

36
00:02:30.498 --> 00:02:34.436
Press ownership affects editorial direction.

37
00:02:35.136 --> 00:02:38.009
Your turn. Say: press ownership.

38
00:02:40.709 --> 00:02:41.873
press ownership.

39
00:02:42.883 --> 00:02:45.336
citizen journalism.

40
00:02:46.036 --> 00:02:52.052
citizen journalism means news reported by ordinary people, often on social media.

41
00:02:52.352 --> 00:02:56.082
Citizen journalism spread news of the flood quickly.

42
00:02:56.782 --> 00:03:00.270
Your turn. Say: citizen journalism.

43
00:03:02.970 --> 00:03:04.772
citizen journalism.

44
00:03:05.783 --> 00:03:07.945
Now let's listen to a conversation.

45
00:03:08.245 --> 00:03:11.859
Ms. Sarah, does it matter who owns a newspaper?

46
00:03:13.150 --> 00:03:17.683
Very much so. To begin with, owners can shape what is reported.

47
00:03:18.983 --> 00:03:22.144
Even if journalists have editorial independence?

48
00:03:23.450 --> 00:03:29.864
In theory, no. In practice, however, advertiser influence and owners' interests can matter.

49
00:03:31.150 --> 00:03:33.277
What about public broadcasters?

50
00:03:34.583 --> 00:03:39.883
They're designed to serve citizens. That said, their funding can make them vulnerable too.

51
00:03:41.183 --> 00:03:43.044
And citizen journalism?

52
00:03:44.350 --> 00:03:50.125
It adds voices. All things considered, media pluralism is the best protection.

53
00:03:51.417 --> 00:03:54.809
Grammar focus: Discourse markers (mastery).

54
00:03:55.109 --> 00:03:59.663
Mastering discourse markers helps you build balanced arguments.

55
00:03:59.963 --> 00:04:05.579
In theory, no. In practice, owners matter. That said, funding can be a weakness.

56
00:04:05.879 --> 00:04:08.737
All things considered, pluralism is best.

57
00:04:09.037 --> 00:04:14.148
Contrasting 'in theory' and 'in practice' is a powerful way to show critical thinking.

58
00:04:15.150 --> 00:04:18.267
Now read and listen. Who Controls the News?.

59
00:04:18.567 --> 00:04:24.897
In many countries, a small number of media conglomerates own most major news outlets.

60
00:04:26.183 --> 00:04:33.547
In theory, journalists enjoy editorial independence; in practice, ownership can influence coverage.

61
00:04:34.850 --> 00:04:40.634
Advertiser influence may also discourage stories that criticise powerful companies.

62
00:04:41.950 --> 00:04:46.562
Public broadcasters aim to serve citizens rather than shareholders.

63
00:04:47.850 --> 00:04:53.170
That said, their funding often depends on governments, which can create other pressures.

64
00:04:54.483 --> 00:04:58.004
State media typically present official perspectives.

65
00:04:59.317 --> 00:05:05.786
Meanwhile, citizen journalism has given ordinary people the ability to report events directly.

66
00:05:07.083 --> 00:05:14.029
All things considered, media pluralism remains the strongest safeguard for an informed public.

67
00:05:15.317 --> 00:05:18.062
Quiz time! Choose the correct answer.

68
00:05:18.362 --> 00:05:21.662
The existence of many different media voices is blank.

69
00:05:21.962 --> 00:05:26.776
A, media pluralism. B, state media. C, press ownership.

70
00:05:30.783 --> 00:05:33.584
The answer is A: media pluralism.

71
00:05:35.083 --> 00:05:39.022
blank, the plan sounds perfect. blank, it is very expensive.

72
00:05:39.322 --> 00:05:45.286
A, In theory / In practice. B, That said / To begin with. C, Ultimately / Even so.

73
00:05:49.283 --> 00:05:52.699
The answer is A: In theory / In practice.

74
00:05:54.183 --> 00:05:56.938
blank, I believe the policy is fair.

75
00:05:57.238 --> 00:06:01.704
A, All things considered. B, In theory. C, That said.

76
00:06:05.717 --> 00:06:09.191
The answer is A: All things considered.

77
00:06:10.683 --> 00:06:13.426
News reported by ordinary people is blank.

78
00:06:13.726 --> 00:06:20.003
A, citizen journalism. B, public broadcaster. C, media conglomerate.

79
00:06:24.017 --> 00:06:27.351
The answer is A: citizen journalism.

80
00:06:28.850 --> 00:06:34.124
According to the text, what is the strongest safeguard for an informed public?

81
00:06:34.424 --> 00:06:39.818
A, state media. B, media pluralism. C, advertising.

82
00:06:43.817 --> 00:06:46.954
The answer is B: media pluralism.

83
00:06:48.450 --> 00:06:54.620
Now it's your turn. Part one: Why does media ownership matter to ordinary citizens?

84
00:06:59.920 --> 00:07:05.565
Part two: Use 'in theory / in practice' and one concluding marker to build your answer.

85
00:07:11.865 --> 00:07:26.423
In theory, any outlet can report freely. In practice, owners and advertisers shape what we see. That said, social media adds new voices. All things considered, citizens need many sources to stay informed.

86
00:07:26.723 --> 00:07:28.769
That is a well-argued answer!

87
00:07:29.783 --> 00:07:34.920
Great work today! You learned eight new words and discourse markers (mastery).

88
00:07:35.220 --> 00:07:38.917
Today's value is honesty. Always tell the truth.

89
00:07:39.217 --> 00:07:42.230
See you in Unit fifty-one. Goodbye!

90
00:07:42.530 --> 00:07:45.112
Thank you, Ms. Sarah! Bye, everyone!

